  "live_records": {
    "q-37146": {
      "dom_id": "step-q37146",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "Find the global extrema of the function $f(x,y)=(x-1)^{2}+{y}^{2}$ on the closed region  $D=\\{(x,y):{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 9\\}.$ You are given that $f$ has a critical point at  $(1,0)$ and on the boundary of the region the points that could give rise to extreme values are  $(-3,0)$, and $(3,0).$",
      "worked_solution": "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$ \n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points inside the region $D.$ \n\nTo determine the critical points, let's first sketch the region:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14159144/assets/d7864a36585a145e009846b23142372caeff3a68f17c9d1f7d1cf17e642f9543.png)\n\nSince the critical point $(1,0)$ lies inside $D,$ we include that point in the list of candidates for the extrema. \n\n**Step 2.** Find the points on the boundary that could give rise to extreme values.\n\nIn this case, we are *given* the points on the boundary of our region that could potentially be global extrema for $f.$ These are\n\n$$\n(3,0),\\,(-3,0).\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following:  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,0) & =(1-1)^{2}+(0)^{2} \\\\  & =0 \\\\ f(3,0) & =(2)^{2}+(0)^{2} \\\\  & =4 \\\\ f(-3,0) & =(-4)^{2}+(0)^{2} \\\\  & =16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $0$ at the point $(1,0),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $16$ at the point $(-3,0).$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
