    "q-83400": {
      "dom_id": "step-q83400",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Find the global maximum of the function $f(x,y)=2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4x-2y$ on the closed triangular region  $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 2-x\\}.$ You're given that $f$ has a critical point at $(1,1)$ and on the boundary of the triangle the points that could give rise to extreme values are the vertices.",
      "worked_solution": "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$\n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points of $f$ that lie inside the region $D.$ \n\nTo determine the critical points, let's first sketch the region:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14159144/assets/30a739bfccac0a1ccc405b7ce1fd34afd46b5a2f436c852cef0bb38a13ec1e64.png)\n\n \n\nSince the point $(1,1)$ does not belong to the interior of $D$, we do not yet include it in the list of candidates for extrema (it lies on the boundary of $D,$ so we will consider it in the next step).\n\n**Step 2.** Find the points on the boundary that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nIn this case, we are *given* the points on the boundary of our region that could potentially be global extrema for $f.$ These are\n\n$$\n(0,2),\\,(2,0),\\,(0,0),\\,(1,1).\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,2) & =2(0)^{2}+(2)^{2}-4(0)-2(2) \\\\  & =4-4 \\\\  & =0 \\\\ f(2,0) & =2(2)^{2}+(0)^{2}-4(2)-2(0) \\\\  & =8-8 \\\\  & =0 \\\\ f(0,0) & =2(0)^{2}+(0)^{2}-4(0)-2(0) \\\\  & =0 \\\\ f(1,1) & =2(1)^{2}+(1)^{2}-4(1)-2(1) \\\\  & =2+1-4-2 \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $-3$ at the point $(1,1),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $0$ at the points $(0,0),(0,2),$ and $(2,0).$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
